                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1175d2ea-be71-4abb-a546-d5fcda1aa646",
                                                "sense_id": "04563d64-ab68-3106-92cb-ddcdbd2e2bd6",
                                                "text": "I means to goo to th' mop, 'er sez, fur I waants a chahinge.[…]'T wuz to w:Muckley mop 'er went."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "85bd98cf-6f45-4f55-96fc-e7f38a9ce878",
                                                "sense_id": "04563d64-ab68-3106-92cb-ddcdbd2e2bd6",
                                                "text": "The Mop Fairs attracted the attention of moralists. The hiring system was seen as a means to acquire girls for prostitution; although there is no evidence that this occurred in Stratford, where girls plying for hire were generally accompanied by their formidable mothers."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "35aabaea-7129-401a-b0c2-cb1fa9368bc2",
                                                "sense_id": "04563d64-ab68-3106-92cb-ddcdbd2e2bd6",
                                                "text": "Mop Fairs: Today's annual events are the modern version of the old hiring fairs, where people attended seeking employment or to change it. They are named after the practice of hopefully skilled employees carrying tassels, known as mops, in their buttonholes indicating their occupation. Those who had no trade carried a mop head. At the end of the following week, they could change employers or employees, at what was called the Runaway Mop."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
